intended use
------------

This map is intended for use with amide (~N(D)C(=O)~) (smiles notation) groups, specifically those that are found in the protein backbone. In other words, it looks for the amide units formed through linking amino acids.

The goal is to calculate the coupling between two amide groups, which could be located either in the backbone, or side chains.


how to install
--------------

This map needs some installation before it can be used:
- copy this map from github to the pairs folder of your maps directory:
  your/map-directory/Pairs/TrEsp
- Find out the location of your GMAP installation directory. In particular, save the **full** path to the sourcefiles directory (including the sourcefiles directory itself).
- navigate to the src directory within the TrEsp directory
- install the c script:
  - Windows:
    - Just as with GMAP installation, use the x86 (32bit) or x64 (64bit) command line from microsoft visual studio
    - navigate to the src directory of your TrEsp copy
    - run the following command, making sure to point to the path of the sourcefiles directory of your installation (that we looked up earlier)
      cl.exe /LD /Fe: TCC_clib_Win64bit /I\your\GMAP\location\sourcefiles TCC_clib.cpp (or, for absolute files, /I d:\path\to\sourcefiles)
  - Linux:
    - You are already in the correct directory (ProteinAmide_TCC/src) to run the install command. Run the following command, making sure to point to the path of the sourcefiles directory of your installation (that we looked up earlier):
      g++ -fPIC -shared -o TCC_clib_Linux.so -I/your/GMAP/location/sourcefiles TCC_clib.cpp
  - MacOS:
    - You are already in the correct directory (ProteinAmide_TCC/src) to run the install command. Run the following command, making sure to point to the path of the sourcefiles directory of your installation (that we looked up earlier):
      g++ -fPIC -shared -o TCC_clib_MacOS.dylib  -I/your/GMAP/location/sourcefiles TCC_clib.cpp


how to use
----------

This map is not designed and intended for stand-alone use, and should never be requested directly. Instead, it should be used together with the 'ProteinAmide' map, which you should request as the coupling map. The main reason for this is that GMAP cannot identify direct neighbours, and apply a different map for them.

Furthermore, this map is specifically designed for coupling AmideI singles map. It has been tested to work with the AmideBB and AmideSC singles maps, so we recommend to only couple those types of oscillators with this map. Any MD-system requirements for those maps automatically apply here as well.

This map should never be applied to calculate the coupling between two groups that share a common (set of) atom(s). This would result in a distance of 0, which means infinite coupling. If the code detects a distance smaller than 0.01 angstrom, a distance of 1 angstrom will be assumed instead. This assumption is not physically meaningful, however! Therefore, these zero-valued distances should always be avoided. This is done by not choosing this method for the nearest-neighbour couplings.


Available parameters
--------------------

When using this map, the following parameters become available to use in any input parameter source (default file, input file, command line):

None. This map has no custom settings to be used.


warnings that can be raised by this map
---------------------------------------

This map has not added any custom errors.